HOW TO READ THIS FILE

  Plain text, append-only, in execution order. Every step is a command line
  introduced by '$ ', followed by its captured streams and exit status:

      [stdout]  ... verbatim; nothing follows the marker when the stream was empty
      [stderr]  ... verbatim; java and some ls failures write here, not to stdout
      [exit]    ... the command's exit status

  No output is summarised, reflowed or elided. Every claim in this file traces to
  a command whose output is present in it. Nothing is asserted without evidence
  and no measurement is fabricated.

METHOD - THE FOUR PRINCIPLES THIS FILE IS HELD TO

  review_rules returned exactly 'No user rules provided.' for the whole document.
  That is a complete read, corroborated by AAP 0.7 and 0.10.2. No user-specified
  rule governs this file, and the absence is not read as licence to lower the bar;
  enterprise best practice is substituted, held to the AAP's own four principles.

  P1 Verification is independent of the thing verified (AAP 0.1.3). The build is
     not run and then inspected for a download. The download CONDITIONAL is
     evaluated here, before the build, using the wrapper's own extraction and its
     own normalization, so the branch is shown to be unreachable rather than
     observed not to have been taken.
  P2 Reject rather than infer (AAP 0.1.3). Each extracted version token is
     validated as non-empty and well-formed. An empty or unparseable token is a
     halt-worthy INCONCLUSIVE result, never an assumed match.
  P3 Halt-and-report over silent repair (AAP 0.8.1). If the exact Maven were not
     available without a download, this file would carry a HALT naming both the
     detected and the required version. Nothing would be installed, and the
     wrapper would not be allowed to install anything either.
  P4 Verbatim preservation (AAP 0.1.3). Every command's output is carried as
     emitted.

WHY THE WRAPPER'S OWN EXTRACTION AND ITS OWN NORMALIZATION, AND NOTHING ELSE

  A hand-rolled version comparison, or a reading of 'mvn --version' in full, could
  pass while the wrapper's own comparison fails - which would defeat the entire
  purpose of a pre-check. The predicate reproduced below is therefore the literal
  predicate build/mvn evaluates, built from the same pipelines: 'head -n1', the
  two awk programs, and the %03d%03d%03d normalization.

